# ============================================================================
# LLM Atlas — environment configuration
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in only what you need.
#
# ACCESS MODEL
#   • FREE open-source models  → served on an OPERATOR-funded key set below.
#     Set ONE of NVIDIA_API_KEY / OPENROUTER_API_KEY / LOCAL_BASE_URL and every
#     open model becomes free for all end users (no key from them).
#   • CLOSED / paid models     → BYOK. Each user connects their OWN key for any
#     provider in the app (Vault → Providers); it's stored encrypted in their
#     browser and sent per-request. Frontier models work with ZERO server keys
#     configured here.
#   • A user's own key also unlocks that provider's FREE tier for them — a free
#     Groq or Z.ai key raises their ceiling without the operator funding it.
#     See the first-party section below for what each provider's "free" means.
# ============================================================================

# --- NVIDIA NIM (build.nvidia.com) — OPERATOR free-tier (optional) ----------
# Get a key at https://build.nvidia.com/ — hosts Nemotron + many open models.
# Recommended operator key: it serves the free open catalog out of the box.
NVIDIA_API_KEY=
# Override the base URL only if you proxy it. Default shown:
# NVIDIA_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1

# --- OpenRouter — OPERATOR free-tier (optional) ----------------------------
# Get a key at https://openrouter.ai/keys — one key, hundreds of models.
# Optional: only set this if YOU (the operator) want to also serve open models
# via OpenRouter. End users supplying their own key for paid models do NOT
# need this — they paste theirs in the app instead.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# Optional attribution headers (recommended by OpenRouter):
OPENROUTER_SITE_URL=https://llmatlas.xyz
OPENROUTER_SITE_NAME=LLM Atlas

# --- Google AI Studio (Gemini API) — OPERATOR key (optional) ----------------
# Get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey — serves the Gemini and
# Gemma families natively on Google's OpenAI-compatible endpoint.
#
# Worth adding: it has a genuinely free tier, so every Gemini/Gemma route flips
# to "Free" the moment the key is present — no resync and no liveness-probe wait,
# because availability is derived per request (lib/catalog/availability.ts).
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai

# --- Groq — OPERATOR key (optional) -----------------------------------------
# Get a key at https://console.groq.com/keys — Llama, Qwen and GPT-OSS at
# LPU speed on an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.
#
# Also free-tier, and the only working route for a few models whose NVIDIA copy
# hangs (notably Llama 3.3 70B).
GROQ_API_KEY=
# GROQ_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1

# ============================================================================
# FIRST-PARTY MODEL LANES (all optional)
#
# Everything above is an operator key: set it and the model becomes free for
# every visitor. Everything below is a lane a USER can also connect for
# themselves in the app (Vault -> Providers), sent per-request and never stored
# on the server. Setting one here just means the operator funds it too.
#
# Honest summary of what "free" means per provider, verified Sep 2026:
#   RECURRING (free with no expiry, inside the stated limits)
#     Z.ai       - GLM Flash models only. Other GLM tiers bill on the same key.
#     Mistral    - ~1B tokens/month, but ONLY if you opt into training.
#   GRANT (runs out; shown as "Credits" in the UI, never as "Free")
#     DeepSeek   - one-time token grant on a new account.
#     xAI        - $25 signup credit, more if you opt into data sharing.
#     Cerebras   - expiring trial credits, and a card is required.
#     DashScope  - per-model free quota, valid for a limited period.
#   NONE
#     OpenAI     - no free tier. The free lane for GPT is the open-weight
#                  gpt-oss models, which run on NVIDIA / Groq above.
#     Anthropic  - no free tier, and routing a Claude Pro/Max subscription
#                  through a third party is not permitted. A paid API key is
#                  the only supported route.
# ============================================================================

# --- Z.ai (GLM) --------------------------------------------------------------
# https://z.ai/manage-apikey/apikey-list
ZAI_API_KEY=
# ZAI_BASE_URL=https://api.z.ai/api/paas/v4

# --- Mistral -----------------------------------------------------------------
# https://console.mistral.ai/api-keys
MISTRAL_API_KEY=

# --- DeepSeek ----------------------------------------------------------------
# https://platform.deepseek.com/api_keys
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=

# --- xAI (Grok) --------------------------------------------------------------
# https://console.x.ai
XAI_API_KEY=

# --- Cerebras ----------------------------------------------------------------
# https://cloud.cerebras.ai
CEREBRAS_API_KEY=

# --- Alibaba DashScope (first-party Qwen) ------------------------------------
# https://bailian.console.alibabacloud.com/
DASHSCOPE_API_KEY=

# --- OpenAI ------------------------------------------------------------------
# https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- Anthropic (Claude) ------------------------------------------------------
# https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
# The one provider that is not OpenAI-compatible: Atlas speaks the Messages API
# to it (lib/router/protocol.ts).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#
# CAREFUL WITH THIS ONE. `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` is a well-known variable that the
# Anthropic SDK and Claude Code already read, and Atlas resolves every base URL
# as `<PROVIDER>_BASE_URL`. So if it is exported in your shell for another tool,
# Atlas silently inherits it. Observed live during development: the shell had it
# set to the bare root, and Atlas dialled `https://api.anthropic.com/messages`
# instead of `.../v1/messages`. `endpointFor()` now normalises the version
# segment so both spellings work, but if Anthropic requests fail with a 404,
# check `echo $ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` before anything else.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com/v1

# --- Atlas as an MCP server (optional) --------------------------------------
# Expose the Atlas catalog, knowledge graph, cost engine and news corpus to MCP
# clients (Claude Desktop, Claude Code) at POST /api/v1/mcp/server.
#
# Public, READ-ONLY, and public data only: no memory, no projects, no
# conversations. API routes in this app do not authenticate, so anything
# user-scoped here would be readable by anyone who found the URL.
#
# Off unless you set this AND turn on the "Serve Atlas over MCP" flag. A route
# that appears the moment someone deploys is a route nobody decided to expose.
# ATLAS_MCP_SERVER_ENABLED=1

# --- Operator-serve-paid (optional) ----------------------------------------
# By default, closed/paid models are strictly BYOK (each user pastes their own
# OpenRouter key). For a personal / single-operator deploy, set this to "true"
# to let YOUR OpenRouter key above also serve closed/frontier models when a
# user hasn't connected their own. Leave unset/false for public multi-user.
# OPERATOR_SERVE_PAID=true

# --- Local inference (Ollama / vLLM / llama.cpp) ---------------------------
# Point at any local OpenAI-compatible endpoint. No key needed for Ollama.
#
# COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE — uncomment only once something is actually serving
# on that port. `local` is the one provider whose configured-ness is signalled
# by a URL rather than a credential (lib/router/index.ts), and this file is
# meant to be copied verbatim (`cp .env.example .env.local`, README). Shipping
# it live meant a fresh clone reported ONE connected provider, showed a green
# "Connected" badge in /vault, suppressed the "connect a key" banner, offered
# ~52 models as Free, defaulted to one of them, and then failed every request
# against a port with nothing behind it. Every diagnostic in the app said
# healthy. Left commented, a verbatim copy configures nothing and the app tells
# the truth about it.
#
# Ollama's default is shown; vLLM and llama.cpp use their own ports.
# LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# LOCAL_API_KEY=

# --- Persistence & memory (Supabase — optional) ----------------------------
# Conversations, projects, playground experiments, code sessions and semantic
# memory (pgvector) persist to Supabase. The app runs fully in-memory without
# these; saving/recall light up once they're set. Apply the schema in
# supabase/migrations/0001_init.sql first.
#   Project settings → API:
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Server-only service-role key (bypasses RLS; used by API routes, never sent to
# the browser). Keep this secret.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# Optional: distinct URL for server calls (defaults to NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL).
# SUPABASE_URL=

# --- Sign-in providers -------------------------------------------------------
# Which OAuth buttons the sign-in and register pages offer. A provider only
# works once it is enabled in the Supabase dashboard with its own client id and
# secret, so this list is what you have actually configured — leaving it unset
# gives email-only sign-in, which needs nothing beyond the keys above.
#
# Supported: google, github, azure, apple, gitlab, discord
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_PROVIDERS=google,github
#
# Three things have to line up in the Supabase dashboard:
#   1. Authentication → Providers: enable each one, paste its client id/secret.
#      The provider's own consent screen needs the callback
#      https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
#   2. Authentication → URL Configuration: set Site URL, and add
#      http://localhost:3000/auth/callback plus your production /auth/callback
#      to Redirect URLs. Missing this is the usual cause of a silent OAuth
#      failure that looks like the button doing nothing.
#   3. Authentication → Email Templates → Magic Link: include {{ .Token }}
#      alongside {{ .ConfirmationURL }}. One request sends both a link and a
#      six-digit code, but the code only reaches the inbox if the template
#      renders it — without this the OTP step has nothing to verify.
#
# Roles live in profiles.role (migration 0011) and cannot be raised from the
# app. Grant the first admin from the Supabase SQL editor:
#   update profiles set role = 'owner' where email = 'you@example.com';

# --- App ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public base URL (used for share links / metadata).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Model catalog sync ------------------------------------------------------
# The catalog is the live union of what NVIDIA NIM and OpenRouter serve,
# refreshed every 24h. It needs no configuration: with no keys the app ships a
# bundled catalog, and with keys set the sync runs in the background the first
# time a served snapshot goes stale.
#
# Three triggers, layered. Whichever exists in your environment wins.
#   1. On-read (always on)  — a request that finds the snapshot older than the
#      interval below serves it immediately and refreshes behind the response.
#   2. Vercel Cron          — vercel.json already declares a daily job. Set
#      CRON_SECRET in the Vercel project so the endpoint accepts it.
#   3. GitHub Actions       — .github/workflows/catalog-sync.yml. Set the repo
#      secrets ATLAS_BASE_URL and CATALOG_SYNC_SECRET.
#
# Shared secret for POST/GET /api/v1/catalog/sync. With neither this nor
# CRON_SECRET set, that endpoint returns 404 and only trigger (1) is active.
# CATALOG_SYNC_SECRET=
# CRON_SECRET=

# Hours before a served snapshot is stale enough to trigger a background refresh.
# ATLAS_CATALOG_SYNC_INTERVAL_HOURS=24

# Blended $/Mtok ceiling below which an *open-weight* synced model is served on
# YOUR operator key instead of the user's own.
#
# Default 0 — only NVIDIA-NIM-routed models and OpenRouter's genuinely $0 tiers
# are "free"; everything else is BYOK. Raising this promotes hundreds of
# open-weight models onto your OpenRouter credit, and you pay for every user's
# tokens. Raise it deliberately.
# ATLAS_FREE_OPEN_CEILING_PER_M=0

# Emergency stop for the background refresh (the secured endpoint still works).
# ATLAS_CATALOG_SYNC_DISABLED=true

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FREE-TIER QUOTA LEDGER
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Atlas counts how much of each operator-funded free tier has been spent, and
# SKIPS a lane whose window is used up rather than dialling it and collecting a
# 429. The limits themselves come from lib/catalog/providers.ts and are
# reconciled against whatever each provider reports in its response headers —
# the table is a starting estimate, the provider is the authority.
#
# There is nothing to switch on. Two things change how well it works:
#
#   * WITH Supabase configured (above), counters are shared, so several server
#     instances running one operator key agree. Apply
#     supabase/migrations/0015_usage_ledger.sql first.
#   * WITHOUT it, counters live in each instance's memory: correct for a single
#     instance, under-counting across several, forgotten on restart. Fine for
#     local and single-box deploys, which is why nothing here is required.
#
# A user's OWN key is never metered here — that quota is theirs, and when an
# operator lane runs out Atlas falls back to their key rather than refusing.
#
# Burst cap per caller on /api/v1/router/chat, for requests that spend YOUR
# key. Before this the route had no limit at all: any anonymous POST spent the
# operator's provider budget. Requests carrying the user's own credential are
# not capped. Spoofable and per-instance — a mitigation, not authentication.
# ATLAS_CHAT_BURST=40

# Snapshots persist to Supabase when it is configured (see above), which lets
# every server instance share one catalog and survive restarts. Apply
# supabase/migrations/0003_catalog_snapshot.sql. Without Supabase the sync still
# runs, but each cold start begins from the bundled catalog.

# --- Atlas News ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The news feed is the live union of ~37 curated AI RSS/Atom sources (first-party
# lab blogs, arXiv, press and analysts), re-synced every hour.
#
# IT NEEDS NO CONFIGURATION. Every feed is a public endpoint with no API key, and
# the first request to a stale corpus schedules a refresh behind the response.
# Everything below is optional tuning.
#
# Four layered triggers, same shape as the catalog sync:
#   1. On-read (always on)  — a request that finds the corpus older than the
#      interval serves it immediately and refreshes behind the response.
#   2. Vercel Cron          — vercel.json declares a DAILY job at 04:07 UTC. Set
#      CRON_SECRET (or NEWS_SYNC_SECRET) so the endpoint accepts it. Hourly crons
#      require a paid Vercel plan, so on Hobby this is daily and triggers 1 and 3
#      carry the rest.
#   3. GitHub Actions       — .github/workflows/news-sync.yml, at :17. Set the
#      repo secrets ATLAS_BASE_URL and NEWS_SYNC_SECRET.
#   4. The Refresh button   — public and rate-limited, see below. Needs no secret.

# Shared secret for POST/GET /api/v1/news/sync. Falls back to CATALOG_SYNC_SECRET
# and CRON_SECRET. With none of the three set, that endpoint returns 404 and only
# triggers (1) and (4) are active.
# NEWS_SYNC_SECRET=

# Minutes before a served corpus is stale enough to trigger a background refresh.
# ATLAS_NEWS_SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60

# The floor between real upstream sweeps, however many refreshes are requested.
# This is what bounds the public Refresh button: no matter how many people click
# it, the ~37 publishers see at most one sweep per window. Inside the window the
# endpoint answers instantly with the next eligible time and does no work.
# ATLAS_NEWS_MIN_REFRESH_MINUTES=10

# Set to "false" to remove the public Refresh button and 404 its endpoint. The
# scheduled triggers keep working.
# ATLAS_NEWS_PUBLIC_REFRESH=true

# How long an article stays in the corpus, and the hard cap on its size. The cap
# drops the lowest-ranked, not the oldest — retention already handles age.
# ATLAS_NEWS_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# ATLAS_NEWS_MAX_ARTICLES=600

# Feeds polled in parallel, and the wall-clock ceiling for the whole fetch stage.
# Feeds are fetched first-party first, so if the budget expires you still have
# the announcements rather than only the commentary about them.
# ATLAS_NEWS_FEED_CONCURRENCY=8
# ATLAS_NEWS_FETCH_BUDGET_MS=45000

# Include Google News aggregator queries. Off by default: their links are
# news.google.com redirects, which cannot satisfy the publisher-domain check, so
# every item from them reads as unconfirmed and none may lead a story cluster.
# ATLAS_NEWS_AGGREGATORS=true

# Emergency stop for all news syncing (the secured endpoint stops too).
# ATLAS_NEWS_SYNC_DISABLED=true

# Optional LLM pass. When enabled AND an operator key exists, each sync rewrites
# a budgeted number of extractive summaries into short neutral abstracts, labelled
# as machine-generated in the UI. Everything the feature promises — verification,
# clustering, topics, entity links — is already true without this; it only adds
# prose. Defaults to a local endpoint when one is configured, because silently
# spending paid credit on hourly cosmetic rewrites is not a sane default.
# ATLAS_NEWS_LLM=true
# ATLAS_NEWS_LLM_BUDGET=24
# ATLAS_NEWS_LLM_MODEL=

# Optional keyed news providers, layered on top of the RSS sources when present.
# Neither is required, and neither replaces the curated feeds.
# NEWSAPI_KEY=
# GNEWS_API_KEY=

# The corpus persists to Supabase when it is configured (see above), which lets
# every server instance share one feed and survive restarts. Apply
# supabase/migrations/0004_news_snapshot.sql. Without Supabase the sync still
# runs, but each cold start begins empty until the first sweep completes.
